WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.830
Seeing fit: ease, drag lines and an accurate fitting.

2
00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:09.020
This is the video for module 1 of Fashion Basics and Clothing Alterations.

3
00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:18.060
Most clients walk in with a feeling: it is not sitting well. Your first job is to turn that into a clear problem you can measure.

4
00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:21.820
Then write exact amounts on the job card.

5
00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:27.600
You will also learn a repeatable fitting routine, and how to advise honestly.

6
00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:40.350
Ease is the difference between the body and the garment at the same place. If her bust is 96 cm and the blouse measures 104 cm, the blouse has 8 cm of ease.

7
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:50.850
Wearing ease is the minimum room to breathe, sit and move. A common guide for a fitted woven garment is about 2.5 cm at the waist.

8
00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:59.510
Design ease is extra room added on purpose, like in a kaftan. Take it all out and it pulls at the hips when she sits.

9
00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:02.780
So always ask how she will wear it.

10
00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:14.080
A drag line is a fold or wrinkle, and its direction points to the cause. Horizontal pulling lines across the hips of a skirt mean the hip is too tight.

11
00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:19.320
Vertical folds at the back waist of a dress mean too much width there.

12
00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:30.920
Diagonal lines point to the part that needs more or less length or width. Look first, then measure, then decide. Never pin before you understand the cause.

13
00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:43.070
For example, Halima, a bank officer in Kaduna, says her fitted dress hangs like a sack at the waist. Lay the dress flat, close it, measure across and double the number.

14
00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:47.790
The bust has 6 cm of ease. That is normal wearing ease, so leave it.

15
00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:53.780
The waist has 18 cm of ease, far more than the 2.5 cm guide.

16
00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:58.380
The hip has 6 cm, comfortable for sitting at a desk.

17
00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:10.960
She sits all day, so you agree about 4 cm of waist ease. 80 plus 4 is 84. So 14 cm comes out at the waist, tapering to nothing at bust and hip.

18
00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:15.770
Write that exact amount on the job card, not a vague note.

19
00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:23.630
The fitting is the most important 15 minutes of the job. Ask her to bring the shoes and underwear she will wear.

20
00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:32.430
Look before you pin. Then ask her to sit and raise her arms. Some garments look perfect standing and pull badly sitting.

21
00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:42.710
Pin from the top down, with the hem last, because changes higher up can change the length lower down. Pinch the same amount on left and right.

22
00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:49.890
Let her look in the mirror and agree. Then read your fitting card back to her, and send her a photo of it.

23
00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:56.470
Floors and bodies are not perfectly level, so do not fold up the same amount all round.

24
00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:03.670
Measure a fixed height from the floor, with a pin every 8 to 10 cm. You move, she stands still.

25
00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:13.730
For example, Tunde's trousers drag. With his shoes on, the new inside leg is 76 cm instead of 82, so you shorten by 6 cm.

26
00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:24.200
Give her a private space to change. Tell her before you touch her, and keep your hands on the garment. A child's parent or guardian stays in the room.

27
00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:29.540
Count your pins out and back in, and sweep the floor with a magnet after every session.

28
00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:41.240
Every alteration moves lines. A hem ending at the narrowest part of the leg usually looks lighter. Describe what the garment does, never criticise her body.

29
00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:52.770
For example, Grace wants her pencil skirt at the knee, which would remove the slit. You pin knee and midi lengths. She sees both and chooses midi, with confidence.

30
00:03:54.020 --> 00:04:03.390
If a seam holds only 1 cm, or satin will show old needle holes, say so before you start. An honest no keeps your reputation.

31
00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:16.950
To remember. Ease is garment minus body. Pulling means tight, bagging means loose. Fit in her event shoes and pin top down. Mark hems from the floor. Write numbers she confirms.

32
00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:26.750
Check the ease on two of your own garments, run a mock fitting with a friend and fill in a fitting card, and pin two styling options to compare.

33
00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:29.040
Continue with the lessons below.
